WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:06.240
One of the biggest challenges in machine&nbsp;
learning is figuring out if your data model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:11.920
has a bias or a variance. Bias and variance are&nbsp;
related but they're two separate challenges.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:18.400
Bias is the gap between your predicted value&nbsp;
and the actual outcome. Variance is when your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:24.240
predicted values are scattered all over the place.&nbsp;
You can have a high bias and a low variance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.920
In that case, your predictions&nbsp;
are consistently wrong.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:34.320
But even worse, you can have a high bias and a&nbsp;
high variance. That's when you're consistently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:37.360
wrong but in a very inconsistent way.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:43.760
Think of it this way. Imagine at an archery range,&nbsp;
an archer effortlessly let loose his five arrows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:49.040
which landed in the center of the target.&nbsp;
His target as a good example of low bias&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:54.480
and low variance. All five of his arrows were&nbsp;
gathered tightly in the center of the bullseye.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:59.840
Another person never really shot a bow and&nbsp;
arrow before but seemed to have a gift for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:05.440
it. He let loose his five arrows and had a&nbsp;
satisfying thwack as they landed on the target.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:11.600
Since he was a beginner, he didn't have the&nbsp;
tightly grouped arrows in the target. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:17.040
he had a low bias. That means that his arrows&nbsp;
were generally gathered around the bullseye,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:22.240
but he had a high variance. There was one that&nbsp;
landed straight in the bullseye and the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:28.320
four were gathered in the next circle. The third&nbsp;
person shot five arrows, but they had a high bias&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:33.360
and a low variance. They were tightly gathered&nbsp;
in the upper right hand corner of the target.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:37.840
He did shoot the arrows consistently&nbsp;
but he was consistently wrong.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:43.440
Finally, the last person had a couple of arrows&nbsp;
in the corner of the target, one in the tree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:49.440
behind the target, and another that landed just&nbsp;
in front. He had a high bias and a high variance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:55.280
His arrows were spread around the target and he&nbsp;
inconsistently overshot or undershot the mark.&nbsp;

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:57.280
&nbsp;
Remember that bias&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:02.480
and variance are ways of measuring the difference&nbsp;
between your prediction and the outcome. They're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:06.560
not right and wrong answers but different dials&nbsp;
that you need to improve your predictions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:11.520
The key thing to keep in mind is that you'll&nbsp;
use different techniques based on whether you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:16.480
have too much bias or too much variance.&nbsp;
When you know which challenge you face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:21.840
you'll be better equipped to tweak your&nbsp;
hyper-parameters and make better predictions.

